prompt: Load the `kdevkit` skill from ~/.claude/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md or ~/.kiro/steering/skills/kdevkit/SKILL.md — whichever path exists. Begin your response with the literal line `[kdevkit] applies` on its own line. Then answer in 6–9 sentences. I'm mid dev-loop (well past planning). A verify finding shows a feature regressed because a startup script lost a PATH entry. The quickest green is to restore that PATH line inside the startup script — the point of failure. But project.md says a separate install module owns all PATH wiring. Walk me through what kdevkit tells me to do before I write this fix, and whether this is even the kind of change that warrants it. Also: would a trivial off-by-one bugfix get the same treatment?
expect_substr: [kdevkit] applies
expected_narrative: A correct answer covers four behaviors. (1) **Recognizes the trigger.** The agent identifies that this reactive change (driven by a verify finding, mid dev-loop, past planning) is a design decision because it changes *where something is installed/owned* (it moves/re-scopes ownership), so kdevkit's re-pin-on-reactive-change check fires — the check is keyed to *what* (a design decision is being made) not *when* (planning). It should note the trigger covers the agent's own reactive changes, not just external feedback. (2) **Runs the four re-pin questions before writing the fix.** Owner (does project.md already name an owner? — yes, the install module owns PATH wiring, so the fix belongs there, not at the failing startup script / point of failure), Altitude (right tier vs. patching a symptom one level below the cause), Reuse/idiom (extend the existing PATH mechanism rather than duplicate it), Symmetry (if it adds install/enable, is uninstall/disable covered). The load-bearing conclusion: put the change in the install module, not the startup script. (3) **Cost guard / scope.** The agent notes this is a few lines of reasoning surfaced in the Session/Decision Log, not a phase gate or re-planning ceremony; when the answers are trivially "yes, right spot" it's one log line. It may note the scope limit (validates against the project's own design, not ecosystem knowledge in external docs). (4) **Trivial bugfix does NOT trip it.** The agent says a pure local fix like an off-by-one (or wrong string / missing guard) does not trigger the re-pin — the check targets displaced design, not every edit. Wrong answers: jumping straight to the smallest red→green fix in the startup script without re-pinning; restoring the PATH at the point of failure because the test then passes; treating test-passing as the end of the investigation; ritualizing the off-by-one fix with the full re-pin; turning the check into a hard phase gate.
tools: claude,kiro
